WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.120
In 2017, the Supreme Court legalized sports&nbsp;
betting. It's become lucrative enough that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.760
DraftKings, one of the most popular betting sites,&nbsp;
debuted two new ads during the Super Bowl. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.800
prospect of taxing gambling revenue has state&nbsp;
governments all over the country queuing up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.880
take full advantage to supplement their budgets.&nbsp;
But who will really benefit? Consider New York:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.240
Governor Andrew Cuomo, ever the central planner,&nbsp;
wants to use the state lottery commission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:40.240
to license just one gambling site that would&nbsp;
agree to share the most revenue with the state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.240
That means New York bettors won't&nbsp;
be able to choose from among the 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.160
major online sports betting services. It means&nbsp;
the state cares more about tax revenue than giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.640
bettors the best odds, and it means a state budget&nbsp;
ever more addicted to one more regressive sin tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.080
like the lottery itself. It's neither good for&nbsp;
bettors nor a healthy way to balance a bloated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:09.040
state budget. Other state governments beware.&nbsp;
Do you think state government should tax online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.120
sports betting? Let us know in our poll. Also let&nbsp;
us know what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:22.480
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

